WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.700
what should Democrats be doing
in order to win in the south?

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:00.767
BILL: Mayor Mitch Landrieu,

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.402
-Oh, good question.
-MITCH: Tell the truth.

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:03.802
BILL: Tell the truth?

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:06.139
Tell the truth. It's important.
You know, when--

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.541
You don't have to go to
the far left,

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.109
you don't have to go to
the far right.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:12.345
Mayors, we solve problems.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.513
And I think people kinda look
at Congress and say,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.982
"How come you can't solve
any problems?"
and that's what we do.

00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:18.918
And people are not
as ideologically bent,

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:21.854
I believe, in the country,
as we seem to think
that they are.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.723
And I just think you gotta
get things done, figure it out--

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.459
If we only had a country
run by all women mayors.

00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:29.995
-That's true. I'm for that.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:31.798
-Then we'd have everything...
-I'm for that,

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.733
But we're gonna have a fight
in the Democratic party,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.269
like the Republicans are gonna
have, about center, left, right.

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:40.273
It's gonna be a battle royale,
and it will prepare us for
the future.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:44.410
Alright. Chris Hayes,
what do you say to people
that claim that MSNBC

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.679
is the liberal equivalent
to Fox News?

00:00:46.812 --> 00:00:49.582
I have defended your network
many times on that point,

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:50.683
it makes me crazy.

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:52.251
And that-- Well, because,

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:55.354
you know, Fox News does not
stick to reality.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:59.225
MSNBC is liberal-leaning,
but they stick to reality.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:00.726
But I'll let you
answer that question.

00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:03.028
What do you say to people?
And that your network

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:05.398
only focuses on Trump
for profits?

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:09.602
Um... So, I'll take
the first question,

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.472
which is that the way
to understand Fox News

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:15.275
is it was built by a very unique
man named Roger Ailes...

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.744
-BILL: Yeah.
-...who had a foot
in two different worlds.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.814
He was a TV, show business guy,
and he was a politics guy.

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:24.517
This is a guy who advised
Richard Nixon,
he advised George H. W. Bush.

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:26.920
What he was building,
and the owner of that network,

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.287
Robert-- Rupert Murdoch,

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:30.956
was explicitly a political
project.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.992
That was the goal from
the beginning.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:36.028
It was an ideological project,
it was a political project,

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:37.096
it was a media project.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.365
That has never been the case
at MSNBC.

00:01:39.665 --> 00:01:42.502
It's never been the case.
There is no Roger Ailes
at MSNBC.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:43.803
It was never... Believe me.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.205
It was not built by any
Ailes-like figure.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:49.175
It ended up being where it is
in prime-time largely because

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.844
-it found an audience for that.
-BILL: Right, it wasn't
originally...

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:54.380
-It was a reflection-- Yes.
-Lou Dobbs was on it, remember?

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:57.149
Yeah, and it was
a reflection of the fact

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.985
that, by hook and by crook,
a market demand was found,

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:02.622
which brings us to the second
point, that you focus on Trump
for ratings.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.657
Donald Trump's the President
of the United States,

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:06.759
and it turns out,
people really wanna...

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:09.228
hear about what the President
of the United States is doing.

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:10.896
-And there's only so much...
-Yes.

00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:13.499
...that you're gonna apologize
for covering the President
of the United States.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.901
BILL: Right. And I'll...

00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:17.804
-(APPLAUSE)
-Yes, and...

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:21.607
Everybody's ratings are up
due to Donald Trump,

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:24.444
and by the way, he loves that,
and takes credit for it.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.379
-CHRIS: Right.
It's his favorite thing.
-I mean, he was just--

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:30.115
-Did you see him in Pennsyl--?
-Except he doesn't want anyone
else to make money on the fact

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:33.986
that they're using his image
and persona to make money.

00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:36.256
He wants to figure out how it
can all come to him.

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:39.158
Did you see him in Pennsylvania
before the special election,

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.626
-when he did the robot?
-Yeah.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.897
And he was saying,
"You'd hate it if I was boring!"

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.433
-(MONA SIGHS) You know--
-As if the most important thing
is that the emperor

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:49.369
-toss bread and circuses out...
-MONA: Which...

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.604
-BILL: ...to the hoi polloi.
-Absolutely. And in fact,

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.006
-that's how he picks people.
Apparently, he liked Bolton...
-CHRIS: Yeah.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.441
-BILL: Yes.
-...because he's interesting,

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.976
as opposed to McMaster,
who was boring.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.010
-BILL: Boring!
-Yeah.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.812
(LAUGHS)

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:03.082
BILL: Alright, Mona, in 2003,
you called liberals

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:04.851
"useful idiots of the Russians."

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:07.453
Would you say it applies
to Republicans and Trump now?

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.989
-(LAUGHS) Okay, so,
first of all--
-BILL: Did you quote--?

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:13.192
So, the title of my book was
"Useful Idiots." It was...

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.161
-BILL: It's in the title!
-...a term of art, okay?

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:19.065
It was the quote from
Vladimir Lenin, who said that

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.567
liberals in the West would be
useful idiots for us,

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:24.303
and we could tell them all kinds
of lies and they'd repeat them,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.506
and you know, so that was what
my point was.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.175
I think it's still valid.
And I think, yes,

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:30.809
it does apply to some
Republicans today.

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:33.513
-BILL: And Trump, especially.
-And Trump. Regarding Trump,
yeah it does.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.647
-Totally a useful idiot.
-MONA: Not all, though!
There's some--

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:37.583
You know what? I just want
to say this on behalf

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.852
of the Republican party,
since I've been hard
on them tonight.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.888
I do think that we can
either save

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:46.225
this great American institution,
or we can turn the page.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.494
And I think we should save
the Republican party.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:52.565
Well-- Or maybe,
I keep saying, go back
to Republican Classic.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.867
-You know. I think
they should run--
-MONA: Yeah. Yeah, right.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.002
Not the New Coke.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.704
BILL: No, not the new one.
They need a Mitt Romney.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:00.673
Somebody who's... You know,
Mitt Romney's looking

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.141
-pretty good now.
-MONA: Yeah.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:03.910
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:04.443 --> 00:04:06.546
Uh... Gina, are there any
EPA regulations

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:08.748
that you would be in favor
of repealing?

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.349
That's a really good question.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.385
Yeah, the ones about those
garage door openers...

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:15.454
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.423
CHRIS: She's running!
She's running!

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:19.825
-MITCH: She's running!
She is, Gina!
-CHRIS: She's running!

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.194
-(APPLAUSE)
-She's running.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:24.063
-I'd like to make
an announcement.
-CHRIS: Yeah!

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:25.365
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:28.534
Well, I don't love every
regulation.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:32.271
-You only regulate when you feel
like there's a need to do that.
-BILL: Right.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:34.340
When somebody is harming
somebody else,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.976
-and you need to resolve
a market failure.
-Yes.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.712
So, yeah, there are some that
should go away,

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:42.448
and we look at them, we are
required to look at them,

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:44.217
like, every five or eight years.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:45.985
You look at them,
you make changes.

00:04:46.251 --> 00:04:47.686
You try to keep up to date.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.855
But what you don't do
is go in with a list

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.724
of things you want to get rid of

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:54.827
-regardless of knowing anything
about them or looking at them...
-CHRIS: Yeah.

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:58.363
...and do it for the sole
purpose of reducing
business cost

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.299
to certain constituencies.

00:05:00.432 --> 00:05:02.001
-That's not what you do.
-(APPLAUSE)

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:03.002
-Okay.
-CHRIS: True.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.305
-Wicked-good answer.
-Thank you.

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:08.540
-MITCH: "Wicked..." (LAUGHS)
-It was wicked!

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:09.976
How... (LAUGHS)

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:15.581
How will Trump's
trade war de-stabilize
US foreign relations?

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:19.451
You know, he kinda went back on
the steel one, right?

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:22.121
-And then he put ones
on Chinese...
-CHRIS: Well, it's a great--

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.990
It's just like a mean girl
with a shit-list.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:25.457
There's no rhyme or--

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:27.993
The steel tariffs is
a great example

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.697
of very efficient means
of producing corruption.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:33.632
-BILL: Right. (LAUGHS)
-Which is to say, "I announce
a blanket tariff,

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.435
-but I'll make some one-on-one
side deals with everybody."
-MONA: True.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.537
Like, "Maybe you stay
at the Trump Hotel,

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.873
and maybe you hook up
my son-in-law
with some financing,

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:42.475
and maybe we can work out--"

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.745
-It really is a classic means
of using the state...
-BILL: Yeah.

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:48.046
...to produce corruption.
And now, we've got all these

00:05:48.146 --> 00:05:50.482
individual bi-lateral deals.
They're gonna carve it out.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.785
Ultimately, what it's going
to end up with is,

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.754
China's gonna sort of
punch us back.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.323
Right now, we are not in
a trade war.

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:59.692
We're at the beginnings of
something that might become it.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:01.593
Right now, it's fairly
penny ante...

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:05.631
but if it gets worse,
it's gonna have real,
real consequences.

00:06:05.697 --> 00:06:09.635
I can't wait for the Trump
voter to walk into Walmart,
and there's nothing there.

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:10.836
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:12.972
Alright. Thank you, everybody.
Thank you, panel.

